@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+**P-254. MCP connection lifetime under `disconnect` and drop.** Phase 9 review (correctness 1-4). An agent dropped after a tools-list refresh kept the connection (and a stdio child) alive through a strong cycle `Inner -> on_tools_changed -> queued McpTool -> Inner`; a `disconnect()` between `start_connect`'s spawn and the task's first poll left the client `Connected`; a debounced tools refresh pending at `disconnect()` reconnected the client about 300 ms later; a panicking `on_tools_changed` callback left `running` set, so refreshes stopped for good, and the `pending` check and the `running` reset were two critical sections. TS reads the generation synchronously in `_connect`, the MCP SDK clears its list-changed debounce timers on `close()`, and `_handleToolsChanged` logs a throw and resets the flag in `finally`. Choice: the agent's callback holds the change queue weakly (the hook owns it); `start_connect` reads the generation before spawning and `establish` stores `Connected` or `Failed` only if the generation is unchanged, under the `state` lock that `disconnect` bumps the generation in; `disconnect` also bumps the tools-changed ticket, and a refresh lists over the live peer only (no lazy connect; skipped when not connected); the callback runs under `catch_unwind` and a panic logs `client=<..>, error=<..> | failed to refresh tools after toolsChanged notification`; a drop guard clears `running`, and the final `pending` check and the reset share one critical section. TS reference: `mcp/client.ts:596,618-625,755-774`; `@modelcontextprotocol/client` `_resetConnectionState`. Files: `src/mcp/agent_tools.rs`, `src/mcp/client.rs`.
+
+**P-255. MCP progress is routed on the receive path; P-255 amends P-244.** Phase 9 review (correctness 5). `rmcp` hands every notification to its own spawned task, so on a multi-thread runtime `on_progress` callbacks ran out of order, and some ran after the response reached `call_once`, which had already removed the route, so they were lost. TS delivers progress in order and before the result. Choice: every transport is wrapped in a private `ProgressTap` (`transport::serve`, used by the factory, stdio, SSE and OAuth paths) whose `receive` delivers `notifications/progress` inline, before the message reaches `rmcp`'s service loop (which still sees it, for `reset_timeout_on_progress`); `McpHandler` no longer implements `on_progress`. Delivery runs under the progress-routes lock, so notifications held because they arrived before their call registered and live ones keep arrival order. TS reference: `mcp/client.ts` `_buildProgressHandler`. Files: `src/mcp/transport.rs`, `src/mcp/client.rs`, `src/mcp/handler.rs`.
+
+**P-256. The `tools/call` input-required driver follows the MCP SDK; P-256 amends P-249.** Phase 9 review (correctness 6, parity 2, parity 3). The Rust loop sent at most 10 requests and dropped the 10th answer, gave every round a fresh `max_total_timeout`, never paced a `requestState`-only round, used its own error texts, sent no round progress, and handed the elicitation callback a signal nothing cancelled while awaiting it outside the call's cancel. The SDK `runInputRequiredDriver` fulfils up to `maxRounds` (10) rounds after the first response and fails when round 11 would start (11 requests, 10 fulfilments), shrinks one `maxTotalTimeout` across legs, sleeps 250 ms (abortable) before a leg that carries only `requestState`, reports `Fulfilling input required by 'tools/call' (round N)` to `onprogress`, and links the caller's abort signal into each round's handler signal. Choice: the same driver. Texts: `Multi-round-trip request 'tools/call' still required input after 10 rounds (inputRequired.maxRounds)`, `Maximum total timeout exceeded` (checked after each round's fulfilment when the call has a `timeout`; the remaining time is the next leg's `max_total_timeout`), `Cannot fulfil input request '<key>': no handler is registered for '<method>' on this client. Declare the corresponding capability and register a handler, or handle input_required results manually.` (also for sampling and roots, which the TS client registers no handler for), and `Invalid result for tools/call: input_required carries neither inputRequests nor requestState (every input_required result must include at least one of the two)`, all `McpErrorKind::Sdk`. A round's elicitations run concurrently with one `ElicitationContext` signal that is cancelled when the call's signal fires or a sibling fails; the round is raced against the call's signal and returns the abort error at once (TS waits until the callback settles; see the divergence row). Round progress goes to the call's progress callback (per-call, else the client's). TS reference: `@modelcontextprotocol/client` `dist/src-WCy6ifGf.mjs:5130-5273,6484-6560`. Files: `src/mcp/client.rs`.
+
+**P-257. MCP auth headers and client-credentials renewal.** Phase 9 review (correctness 7, 8). With `auth` or `auth_provider`, a user `Authorization` header went out next to the bearer token (`reqwest` appends); and a client-credentials token was granted once per connection, so after expiry `rmcp`'s refresh (client-credentials responses carry no refresh token) failed and requests went out unauthenticated until `connect(true)`. TS sets the token with `headers.set("Authorization", ..)`, and its non-interactive provider runs the grant again on a 401. Choice: with `auth` or `auth_provider`, `authorization` is dropped from the custom headers, so the token wins. The url-configured client with `auth` uses a private `StreamableHttpClient` (`mcp::auth::ClientCredentialsClient`) around the granted `AuthClient`: before each request it takes the stored token, or runs the grant again when `rmcp` reports none usable; on a 401 it runs the grant again (once per rejected grant, shared by concurrent requests) and retries once. A freshly granted token is used as granted, without `rmcp`'s 30-second expiry margin, so a server issuing tokens shorter than 30 s still works. TS reference: `@modelcontextprotocol/client` `dist/index.mjs:5444-5452,5740-5752`. Files: `src/mcp/auth.rs`, `src/mcp/client.rs`.
+
+**P-258. MCP prompt and resource lists without the capability; P-258 amends P-248.** Phase 9 review (correctness 9). Only `list_tools` checked the server capability; `list_prompts`, `list_resources` and `list_resource_templates` sent the request and failed with `-32601` against a tools-only server. The MCP SDK returns `{ prompts: [] }` / `{ resources: [] }` / `{ resourceTemplates: [] }` when the capability is missing. Choice: each list method returns the empty (`Default`) result when the connected server lacks `prompts` (prompts) or `resources` (resources and templates). TS reference: `@modelcontextprotocol/client` `dist/index.mjs:3681-3684,3719-3722`. Files: `src/mcp/client.rs`.
